Layout usage per page:

admin/pages/* — @extends('layouts.admin')

admin/posts/create, admin/posts/edit, admin/posts/index — @extends('layouts.admin') (index was <x-app-layout>, converted)

admin/categories/index — <x-app-layout> (still, may be blank)

pages/show, posts/index, posts/show — <x-app-layout>

admin/settings/index — @extends('layouts.admin')

Important gotcha: create.blade.php and edit.blade.php exist in three folders each (pages/, posts/, categories/). VS Code tabs show only the filename, so it's easy to edit the wrong one.

7. CKEditor 5 setup
Install
bash
npm install ckeditor5 @ckeditor/ckeditor5-style @ckeditor/ckeditor5-font \
  @ckeditor/ckeditor5-alignment @ckeditor/ckeditor5-highlight \
  @ckeditor/ckeditor5-list @ckeditor/ckeditor5-source-editing \
  @ckeditor/ckeditor5-show-blocks
All GPL-licensed. No premium packages.

Initialiser — resources/js/ckeditor-init.js
Imports 'ckeditor5/ckeditor5.css' and 'ckeditor5/ckeditor5-content.css' at the top

Uses licenseKey: 'GPL'

Exposes window.initCkEditor(selector, options)

Supports options.simpleUpload → passed to CKEditor for image uploads

Attaches a form submit listener that copies editor content back into the <textarea> before submit

Uses full toolbar: undo/redo, sourceEditing, showBlocks, heading, style, fontSize, fontFamily, fontColor, fontBackgroundColor, bold, italic, underline, link, insertTable, highlight, blockQuote, codeBlock, alignment, lists, indent/outdent

Blade pattern (used in four files)
blade
@push('scripts')
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/build/assets/ckeditor-init-XXXX.css">
<script type="module">
    import('{{ Vite::asset("resources/js/ckeditor-init.js") }}').then(() => {
        window.initCkEditor('#content', {
            simpleUpload: {
                uploadUrl: '{{ route("admin.posts.upload_image") }}',
                headers: { 'X-CSRF-TOKEN': '{{ csrf_token() }}' }
            }
        }).catch(err => console.error('[CKEditor] init failed', err));
    }).catch(err => console.error('[CKEditor] module load failed', err));
</script>
@endpush
Files using this: admin/pages/create, admin/pages/edit, admin/posts/create, admin/posts/edit.

Why <link> + import() and not just <script src>
Vite splits dynamically imported modules into separate JS and CSS chunks. Loading only the JS chunk leaves the CSS unloaded, and the editor renders with oversized unstyled icons. The explicit <link> on the CSS chunk fixes it.

Build output
public/build/assets/ckeditor-init-XXXX.js (~1.1 MB)

public/build/assets/ckeditor-init-XXXX.css (~219 KB)

Hashes change on every npm run build — the hardcoded <link> filename in the four blades must be updated after each rebuild

vite.config.js
js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import laravel from 'laravel-vite-plugin';

export default defineConfig({
    plugins: [
        laravel({
            input: [
                'resources/css/app.css',
                'resources/js/app.js',
                'resources/js/ckeditor-init.js'
            ],
            refresh: true,
        }),
    ],
    optimizeDeps: { include: ['ckeditor5'] },
    build: { commonjsOptions: { include: [/ckeditor5/, /node_modules/] } },
});
8. Settings / theming system
Settings page (admin/settings/index.blade.php)
Native <input type="color"> fields (no JavaScript library):

settings[primary_color] — default #2563eb

settings[accent_color] — default #f59e0b

settings[text_color] — default #1f2937

settings[header_bg_color] — default #ffffff

Plus dropdowns for heading_font, body_font, border_radius, container_width, and a header_image file upload.

Saved via PUT admin.settings.update.

How theming flows
Settings page saves key / value pairs into settings table.

Both layouts (layouts/admin.blade.php and layouts/app.blade.php) read $settings and output CSS variables in a <style> block:

css
:root {
    --primary-color: {{ $primaryColor }};
    --accent-color: {{ $accentColor }};
    --text-color: {{ $textColor }};
    --heading-font: {!! $headingFont !!};
    --body-font: {!! $bodyFont !!};
    --border-radius: {{ $borderRadius }};
}
layouts/admin.blade.php and layouts/app.blade.php both now contain:

css
body {
    color: var(--text-color);
    font-family: var(--body-font);
}
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
    font-family: var(--heading-font);
}
tailwind.config.js was modified so text-gray-700 and text-gray-900 resolve to var(--text-color):

js
colors: {
    gray: {
        50: '#f9fafb',
        100: '#f3f4f6',
        200: '#e5e7eb',
        300: '#d1d5db',
        400: '#9ca3af',
        500: '#6b7280',
        600: '#4b5563',
        700: 'var(--text-color)',
        800: '#1f2937',
        900: 'var(--text-color)',
        950: '#030712',
    },
},
What this achieves: setting a new text_color on the settings page changes body text and headings site-wide (both public and admin), because most of your copy uses text-gray-700 or text-gray-900.

What still doesn't respond to settings: primary_color, accent_color, border_radius, heading_font, body_font are all declared as CSS variables but only text_color and the two font values are actually wired to elements. To activate the others, similar Tailwind-class-to-variable mappings or additional body {} rules would be needed.

9. Everything fixed during this session
CKEditor invalid-license-key — the trial key on the CDN wasn't valid for the generic CDN build. Solution: self-host the GPL build via npm.

window.initCkEditor is not a function — traced through many false leads (race condition, cache, compiled views) to the real cause: the <script type="module" src="..."> tag emitted by @vite wasn't executing. Replaced with an inline <script type="module"> that does a dynamic import('/build/assets/ckeditor-init-XXXX.js').

Stale compiled views in storage\framework\views\ — php artisan view:clear wasn't always enough; needed Remove-Item -Recurse -Force storage\framework\views\*.

Old UMD script still in blades — four editor blades had the previous CDN-era code. Fixed via PowerShell Set-Content overwrites.

layouts/app.blade.php still had a <script src="{{ asset('js/ckeditor-init.js') }}"> — file didn't exist, caused 404s on every page using the layout.

Blank pages on <x-app-layout> pages (admin/posts/index, admin/categories/index, pages/show, posts/index, posts/show) — layout used @yield('content') but <x-app-layout> passes $slot. Changed to {{ $slot }} and added @isset($header).

CKEditor icons huge / toolbar vertical — Vite splits dynamic imports into JS + CSS chunks; only the JS was being imported. Added explicit <link> to the CSS chunk.

$accentColor undefined error — stale compiled view referencing an old version of the layout.

Post edit page loaded the pages edit form — admin/posts/edit.blade.php had $page variables. Overwritten with correct post form.

Global text color setting didn't apply — the CSS variable was declared but no rule used it. Added body { color: var(--text-color); } and wired Tailwind's gray-700/900 to the variable.

layouts/app.blade.php had CDN CKEditor <link>/<script> tags — removed the scripts; the CSS <link>s remain and are harmless.

10. Known remaining issues / rough edges
layouts/app.blade.php still loads two CKEditor CDN stylesheets in its head. Not needed since CKEditor is self-hosted; safe to remove, but left in place during stabilisation.

admin/categories/index.blade.php still uses <x-app-layout>. It renders now (layout is fixed) but is inconsistent with the other admin pages using @extends('layouts.admin').

primary_color, accent_color, border_radius are declarative only — they're output as CSS variables but no element reads them. Buttons on the admin side do use --primary-color via button[type="submit"] { background-color: var(--primary-color) } in layouts/admin.blade.php, but public pages don't.

CSS <link> hash is hardcoded in the four editor blades. Every npm run build changes the hash and breaks the CSS link until it's updated. A manifest-based include would fix this.

Two PageController classes exist (App\Http\Controllers\PageController and App\Http\Controllers\Admin\PageController). Only one is routed. Confusing for maintenance.

layouts/admin.blade.php and layouts/app.blade.php duplicate the settings @php block and the CSS-variable <style>. Could be extracted to a shared partial or a view composer.

The <style> block in both layouts doesn't apply primary_color to actual elements on the public side — nothing uses var(--primary-color) except the admin submit-button rule.

11. Working conventions learned the hard way
PowerShell Set-Content with a here-string (@' ... '@) is the reliable way to write files. VS Code "saves" during this session sometimes landed in the wrong file (three identically-named create.blade.php files across folders) — check the file path, not just the tab name.

After any blade change: php artisan view:clear. If changes still don't appear, Remove-Item -Recurse -Force storage\framework\views\*.

After any npm run build: refresh the browser hard (Ctrl+Shift+R) and update the CKEditor CSS <link> hash in the four editor blades.

When debugging a blank page: View Page Source (Ctrl+U) first, not Console. Laravel fatals show as empty HTML with no console error.

When debugging CSS: right-click → Inspect → Styles panel → find the winning (uncrossed) rule. That's the definitive answer, faster than guessing.

